			templ_7745c5c3_Err = templruntime.WriteString(templ_7745c5c3_Buffer, 1, "<h1>Privacy Policy</h1><p class=\"last-updated\">Last updated: January 2026</p><p>thoop is an open-source terminal client for viewing your WHOOP data. This policy explains how your data is handled.</p><h2>How thoop Works</h2><p>thoop is a lightweight proxy that connects your terminal to the WHOOP API. The thoop server (<code>thoop.fly.dev</code>) facilitates OAuth authentication and proxies API requests to WHOOP on your behalf.</p><h2>Data Storage</h2><h3>On Your Computer (Client)</h3><ul><li><strong>OAuth tokens</strong> (access token, refresh token, API key) are stored in your operating system's secure keyring (macOS Keychain, Windows Credential Manager, or Linux Secret Service)</li><li><strong>WHOOP data</strong> (cycles, sleep, recovery, workouts) is cached locally in a SQLite database in your config directory</li><li><strong>No data is sent anywhere except</strong> to the thoop proxy server and WHOOP's API</li></ul><h3>On the Server</h3><ul><li><strong>WHOOP User ID</strong> - Used to associate your API key with your account</li><li><strong>API key hash</strong> - A one-way hash of your API key for authentication (not the key itself)</li><li><strong>Webhook events</strong> - Metadata about data updates from WHOOP (entity type, action, timestamp)</li></ul><h3>What We Do NOT Store</h3><ul><li>Your WHOOP username or password</li><li>Your OAuth access or refresh tokens</li><li>Your actual WHOOP data (sleep scores, recovery, HRV, strain, etc.)</li><li>Any personal health information</li></ul><h2>Data Flow</h2><p>When you use thoop:</p><ol><li>You authenticate directly with WHOOP via OAuth in your browser</li><li>WHOOP redirects back to the thoop server with an authorization code</li><li>The server exchanges this for tokens and returns them to your local client</li><li>Your client stores tokens securely in your OS keyring</li><li>API requests flow through the thoop server as a proxy to handle rate limiting</li><li>Your WHOOP data is returned directly to your client and cached locally</li></ol><h2>Rate Limiting</h2><p>The server enforces rate limits to comply with WHOOP's API terms and ensure fair usage across all users. Rate limit counters are stored temporarily and contain no personal data.</p><h2>Data Retention</h2><p>thoop caches your WHOOP data locally to respect WHOOP's API by avoiding unnecessary requests. Instead of fetching the same data repeatedly, thoop stores it in a local SQLite database and intelligently refreshes data based on its state:</p><ul><li><strong>Finalized data</strong> (unscorable) - never re-fetched</li><li><strong>Pending scores</strong> - refreshed after 5 minutes</li><li><strong>Today's scored data</strong> - refreshed after 15 minutes</li><li><strong>Historical scored data</strong> - refreshed after 24 hours</li></ul><p>Additionally, thoop receives webhook notifications from WHOOP when your data changes. These notifications contain only minimal metadata (entity type, action, and ID) to signal that a refresh is needed—not your actual WHOOP data. This allows thoop to fetch updates only when necessary, reducing load on WHOOP's servers and ensuring compliance with their rate limiting policies.</p><p>The local cache is stored in the following locations:</p><ul><li><strong>macOS</strong>: <code>~/.config/thoop/thoop.db</code></li><li><strong>Linux</strong>: <code>~/.config/thoop/thoop.db</code></li><li><strong>Windows</strong>: <code>C:\\Users\\&lt;username&gt;\\.config\\thoop\\thoop.db</code></li></ul><p>If you revoke thoop's access through WHOOP's app settings, you should delete the <code>~/.config/thoop</code> directory to remove all cached data. Server-side records (user ID, API key hash) are retained until you request deletion.</p><h2>Data Sharing</h2><p>We do not sell, license, lease, or share your WHOOP data with any third parties. Your data is only transmitted between your local client, the thoop proxy server, and WHOOP's API.</p><h2>Third-Party Services</h2><p>thoop uses the <a href=\"https://developer.whoop.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WHOOP Developer API</a>. By using thoop, you also agree to WHOOP's <a href=\"https://developer.whoop.com/api-terms-of-use/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">API Terms of Use</a> and <a href=\"https://www.whoop.com/privacy/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Privacy Policy</a>.</p><h2>Data Security</h2><ul><li>All communication uses HTTPS encryption</li><li>OAuth tokens are stored only in your OS keyring, never in plaintext</li><li>API keys are stored as one-way hashes on the server</li><li>Webhook signatures are validated using HMAC-SHA256</li></ul><h2>Security Incidents</h2><p>In the unlikely event of a security breach affecting your data, we will promptly notify affected users through GitHub issues and update this page. We will also report incidents to WHOOP as required by their API terms.</p><h2>Your Rights</h2><p>You can:</p><ul><li><strong>View connection status</strong> - thoop displays your authentication state in the app</li><li><strong>Delete all data and revoke access</strong> - Run <code>thoop purge</code> to revoke WHOOP access and delete all local data including credentials, cached WHOOP data, and logs</li><li><strong>Request server data deletion</strong> - Contact the maintainer to remove your server-side record</li></ul><h2>Open Source</h2><p>thoop is open source under the <a href=\"https://github.com/garrettladley/thoop/blob/main/LICENSE\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MIT License</a>. You can inspect exactly how your data is handled in the <a href=\"https://github.com/garrettladley/thoop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source code</a>.</p><h2>Disclaimer</h2><p>THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER LIABILITY.</p><h2>Contact</h2><p>For questions about this privacy policy or to request data deletion, please <a href=\"https://github.com/garrettladley/thoop/issues\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">open an issue</a> on GitHub.</p>")
